- Adapt to 32-bit and compare performance with builtin_ctz.
- How would you verify correctness across all 64-bit inputs efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of deeper bit-manipulation: 3D Morton ordering for spatial indexing, with no loops and tight constants.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (z-order) encoding across 3 dimensions
- bit spreading (part1by2) with masks/shifts
- avoiding branches for tight hot-paths

## Code Example
```javascript
function part1by2(n){
  let x = BigInt(n) & 0x1fffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 32n)) & 0x1f00000000ffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 16n)) & 0x1f0000ff0000ffn;
  x = (x | (x << 8n))  & 0x100f00f00f00f00fn;
  x = (x | (x << 4n))  & 0x10c30c30c30c30c3n;
  x = (x | (x << 2n))  & 0x1249249249249249n;
  return x;
}
function morton3D(x,y,z){
  return part1by2(x) | (part1by2(y) << 1n) | (part1by2(z) << 2n);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to 32-bit inputs? Trade-offs?
- Could you implement a inverse decode that recovers x,y,z from the code?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests low-level bit manipulation and the ability to express 128-bit results without loops.

## Key Concepts

- Bit interleaving (Morton code)
- Bit spreading with masks
- 128-bit composition from two 64-bit halves

## Code Example

```javascript
function interleave64to128(a,b){
  // spreading, combining, returning {hi, lo}
  // exact spread64to128 implementation omitted for brevity
  const spreadA = spread64to128(a);
  const spreadB = spread64to128(b);
  const hi = spreadA.hi | (spreadB.hi << 1n);
  const lo = spreadA.lo | (spreadB.lo << 1n);
  return {hi, lo};
}
```

